n every stack and e2e run, but this is not proven.<br>- **F-DG4-146** (Low; FE): no web unit test pins the F-DG2-570 rule that a page `GET` waits for the `/me` in flight. A mutant that drops the wait passes all unit-web tests; the product does implement the wait.<br>- **F-DG4-147** (Low; FE): `session-end.spec.ts` 'a 403 is not a session end' awaits only `load`. Under load, the SPA's boot `/me` follows the re-login and adopts the new session's CSRF token, so the expected 403 is a 200. The product behaves correctly; the test races.<br>- **F-DG4-148** (Low; FE): at load 13 or more, `pnpm test` fails in 6 of 8 runs. First renders of lazily loaded page modules miss unit-web's 5 s wait, and `architecture.test.ts` misses its 30 s timeout. The `&&` chain then skips the formula no-codegen project.<br>**Decided:**<br>- **F-DG4-128:** the shipped worker passes a bounded supervision cadence of 60 s, which pg-boss documents, configurable through the worker's configuration. A test starts the worker's own `createBoss` path with the shipped defaults and asserts the cadence, not NaN. The test infrastructure that passes 1 s explicitly stays as it is.<br>- **F-DG4-146:** a unit test holds the `/me` answer and asserts that no page `GET` is sent until it is answered. It must fail on a mutant that drops the wait.<br>- **F-DG4-147:** the spec waits for the app's boot `GET /me` response before the re-login, so it tests D-073 deterministically.<br>- **F-DG4-148:** the required unit check is made reproducible under contention without weakening any test. The candidate remedies are warming or prebundling the lazily loaded page modules in the unit-web setup, splitting the chained projects so a unit-web failure no longer skips the formula project, or a per-file first-render budget that is documented and justified. Raising a wait only to hide slowness is not accepted. FE-R10 shows the result with `pnpm test` under a CPU load of 13–16.<br>**Repairs:** BE-R6 (F-DG4-128) and FE-R10 (F-DG4-146 to F-